fix(touch): use the control-register cancel/abort bits, and verify recovery on the device

0118 wrote M_CMD_ABORT (the IRQ status bit 5) into SE_GENI_M_CMD_CTRL_REG,
where cancel is bit 2 and abort is bit 1; 0x20 is a silent no-op, so the
timeout path claimed a recovery it never performed.

0120 separates the two bit families (M_GENI_CMD_CANCEL_REQ/ABORT_REQ for the
control register, M_CMD_CANCEL/ABORT for the status register) and turns
kt_se_abort() into kt_se_recover():

  - if GENI_STATUS bit 0 is clear, just clear watermark and IRQs;
  - otherwise write cancel, wait bounded for cancel/done or idle;
  - if still active, write abort and wait again;
  - clear watermark and IRQs, re-read GENI_STATUS, and only report success
    when the active bit is gone; otherwise mark the SE dead for the run.

kt_i2c_error() keeps the raw status before deciding to recover, and both
transfer entry points refuse to run once the SE is dead.  TOUCHSE (pins,
reset, se_init) and TOUCHPING (one-byte FIFO write to 0x4B) split Gate D
out of the full identify sequence.

Measured with the new image: TOUCHSE completes all four init groups and
M_IRQ_EN reads 0x7fc0007f; TOUCHPING's one-byte write times out with the
command active and no NACK, and the cancel/abort path then brings
GENI_STATUS back to 0 (last status 0x21 = done|abort).  The pins still read
0 in both directions, so the next question is the touch supply, not GENI.

